> @jussisaurio Maintainer of the Sentry middleware here. Is there anything we should do differently? We don't have any more changes lined up as we believe our type definition seems to be correct now. Thanks!

I don't think there's necessarily anything wrong with your implementation. 👍 

---

More generally:

I've been trying to root cause this bug today and although I do have a PR that ostensibly fixes the issue, I'm still not sure what the exact cause is. I think in your (Sentry's) case the `T` in your middleware gets inferred as `Promise<MiddlewareResult<U>>` where `U` is `ProcedureParams<AnyRootConfig, unknown, unknown, unknown, unknown, unknown, unknown>`, and where `AnyRootConfig` is

```
RootConfig<{
  ctx: any;
  meta: any;
  errorShape: any;
  transformer: any;
}>;
```

Given that the user-visible issue seems to be errors with mapping `ctx` correctly between middlewares, my suspicion is that since `any` behaves very bizarrely with conditional types (it becomes a union of both arms of the ternary type), that has something to do with it.

But this is at least 50% conjecture.

Interesting, I'm not quite sure what's going on there, some weirdness related to the intersection type of Context maybe? Had a play around to simplify the Context type and it's still happening, though I have had success in the past with that approach.

We've been playing with a different compromise internally which is more reliable as it's fighting typescript much less, just tested out for this case and it does also work here:

https://stackblitz.com/edit/github-nqqlsq-msqnp8?file=src%2Fserver%2Fmake-trpc.ts,package.json,src%2Fserver%2Fcreate-context.ts

```ts
  type WithAuthOpts = {
    req?: any;
  };
  function withAuth<TNext extends NextFuncHack>(
    next: TNext,
    { req }: WithAuthOpts
  ) {
    if (!req) {
      throw new TRPCError({
        code: 'INTERNAL_SERVER_ERROR',
        message: 'missing req object',
      });
    }
    try {
      return next({
        ctx: {
          // auth,
        },
      });
    } catch (e) {
      throw new TRPCError({ code: 'UNAUTHORIZED' });
    }
  }

  return {
    router: t.router,
    publicProcedure: t.procedure,
    createProtectedProcedure: () =>
      t.procedure.use((opts) =>
        withAuth(
          opts.next,

          // Context is an intersection type and has to be checked, but that's specific to this Context type
          { req: 'req' in opts.ctx ? opts.ctx.req : undefined }
        )
      ),
  };
```

